-- ============================================================================
-- PD5 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Gas Well Performance, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: WHAT THE DESIGN HIDES OR WHAT BREAKS IT. Five of its
-- six graded fields are quantities the design report never prints, and the
-- sixth is the one place the engine and its own oracle disagree.
--
-- CHOSEN ONCE, USED EVERYWHERE. recommendCorrelation takes ONE argument, a
-- wellhead pressure, and switches at a 1000 psia limit. The capstone wellhead
-- is 999.62 psia, so the well is recommended the UNADJUSTED equation by four
-- tenths of a psi, and that recommendation is then applied at a shoe sitting
-- at 2.26 times the limit. The adjustment is exactly 1.2 on every critical
-- rate, so the arithmetic is trivial and the consequence is not: on this well
-- it is the difference between a 2.750 in workover string and a 2.323 in one.
-- The reason string also calls whatever pressure it is handed a "wellhead",
-- and it PRINTS THE THRESHOLD IT JUST CLEARED, because the message is
-- formatted with Math.round: at 999.62 psia it reads "At 1000 psia wellhead".
--
-- A SIZING RETURNS ONE ANSWER AND DISCARDS THE REST. `largestUnloaded` is the
-- first row that clears, and the rejected rows are the rest of the object. On
-- this well the rejected 2.922 in candidate misses by 2.99 percent. A pick
-- that clears in its second decimal and a pick that clears twice over are the
-- SAME FIELD, and only the rows say which one you are holding. The function
-- gained an `ok` key at engines 5733550 for exactly this reason: a null
-- `largestUnloaded` had been carrying two different meanings with one value.
--
-- THE ONE SEAM THE GATE ITSELF RECORDS, AND IT IS GRADED HERE. The slug
-- hydrostatic term carries the platform's ROUNDED 0.433 psi/ft per unit of
-- specific gravity where the oracle uses rho g exactly, 0.4335275. On the
-- capstone slug that is 78.232275 psi against 78.327582, 0.1218 percent apart
-- and 1,162 TIMES the graded tolerance. It is the only graded value in this
-- course a learner can reach with a hand calculation, and doing it by hand
-- with the textbook gradient FAILS. The gate loosens exactly that one
-- assertion to 5e-3 relative and pins the constant.
--
-- TWO FAILS-OPEN, BOTH RECORDED RATHER THAN FIXED BECAUSE plungerLift FEEDS A
-- LIVE SUITE APPLICATION.
--
--   THE GAS REQUIREMENT FALLS AS THE WELL GETS WEAKER, which is the wrong
--   direction. The cycle gas is evaluated between the casing pressure and the
--   pressure still needed at the top of the rise, so when the casing cannot
--   reach the requirement the average runs BACKWARDS and the number keeps
--   coming. The same well at 745, 288 and 96 psia of casing needs 9,306.7,
--   5,362.8 and 3,705.9 scf/bbl, and at BOTH 288 and 96 psia the gas test
--   TURNS TRUE on a well whose casing cannot move the plunger at all.
--
--   AND THE LONGEST LIFTABLE SLUG CLAMPS TO ZERO INSTEAD OF REFUSING. The same
--   well at 96 psia reports 0 ft, which reads as "the longest slug is nothing"
--   when the truth is that the plunger will not move at all. A refusal wearing
--   a number is worse than an error, because it is quotable.
--
-- WHAT NOBODY CHECKS, AND THE TIER ENDS ON IT. A day of cycling this plunger
-- delivers 18.796 bbl/d against the 373.8 bbl/d the well makes, a factor of
-- 19.9. Every pressure and gas test in the screen can pass on an installation
-- that cannot carry the well's liquid, because nothing in the screen ever
-- compares the cycle's DELIVERY to the well's PRODUCTION. `feasible` is a
-- statement about a plunger, not about a well, and the two read alike.
-- ============================================================================

values ('gaswell', 'advanced', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-chosen-once-used-everywhere","title":"Chosen Once, Used Everywhere","lesson_keys":["l01-chosen-at-the-wellhead","l02-used-at-the-shoe","l03-what-twenty-percent-is-worth","l04-a-threshold-cleared-by-nothing","l05-what-choosing-per-station-changes"]},{"key":"m02-the-candidate-that-was-discarded","title":"The Candidate That Was Discarded","lesson_keys":["l01-a-sizing-returns-one-answer","l02-the-rejected-string","l03-reading-a-rejection","l04-what-a-pick-hides"]},{"key":"m03-the-gradient-constant","title":"The Gradient Constant","lesson_keys":["l01-a-rounded-constant","l02-what-the-rounding-is-worth","l03-the-same-seam-elsewhere","l04-which-conventions-travel"]},{"key":"m04-a-requirement-that-falls","title":"A Requirement That Falls","lesson_keys":["l01-gas-against-casing-pressure","l02-the-wrong-direction","l03-a-flag-that-flips-the-wrong-way","l04-the-clamp-at-zero","l05-what-feasible-still-catches"]},{"key":"m05-what-nobody-checks","title":"What Nobody Checks","lesson_keys":["l01-liquid-capacity","l02-the-cycle-against-the-well","l03-two-molecular-weights-of-air","l04-a-sizing-at-any-station","l05-what-a-screen-cannot-tell-you"]},{"key":"m06-the-expert-reading","title":"The Expert Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
